**Topic.** Asking an LLM for “exactly 4 sentences” or “exactly 100
words” often fails. We build one pipeline on a corpus stored in HDFS in
which classic ML and LLMs work together. Stage 1 (Big Data, Spark
MLlib): 3 ML algorithms score every article sentence and select the
top-m. Stage 2 (Text Mining): the selected sentences are injected into
the LLM prompt together with the control instruction, and small
instruction-tuned LLMs are compared on exact length and format
constraints, against prompts with the full article. “Prompt injection”
here means inserting control instructions and selected content into the
prompt; the articles are treated as untrusted data, never as
instructions. All numbers are planned and will be verified at ingestion.

## 1. Dataset & Scale Metrics

Source: CNN/DailyMail v3.0.0 (311,971 news articles with reference
highlights: 287,113 train / 13,368 validation / 11,490 test; Apache 2.0
on the dataset card),
<https://huggingface.co/datasets/abisee/cnn_dailymail>

| Metric           | Planned value (Stage 1)                                                                             | Requirement       |
|------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|
| N (patterns)     | about 700,000 sentences, from a seeded sample of about 25,000 articles (full corpus staged in HDFS) | N ≥ 20,000        |
| p (features)     | 8 (8 numeric, 0 categorical)                                                                        | p ≥ 4             |
| n_A (algorithms) | 3: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient-Boosted Trees                                       | n_A ≥ 3           |
| N × p            | about 5,600,000 (still 4,000,000 even at only 20 sentences per article)                             | N × p ≥ 1,000,000 |

Stage 2 (not counted in N): 50 held-out articles × 3 model systems × 5
prompt strategies × 4 constraint tasks = 3,000 first-pass generations
(to be re-sized after a pilot).

## 2. Regarding p, Categorical Features, Infrastructure and Tools

p = 8 counts raw features only: 8 numeric and 0 categorical, so no
one-hot encoding is involved. The TF-IDF vectors (Spark ML Tokenizer,
StopWordsRemover, HashingTF, IDF) are only an intermediate step to
compute 2 of the 8 scalar features and are not counted in p. Labels come
from the reference highlights, features never use them (no leakage), and
the official article-level splits prevent sentences of one article from
appearing in both train and test.

Everything runs on the course virtual machine (Vagrant + VirtualBox,
Ubuntu 24.04, Java 21, provisioned by the course bootstrap.sh of
2025-12-16) with Hadoop 3.4.2 in pseudo-distributed mode and Spark 4.1.0
in local mode. The notebook downloads the dataset and loads it into HDFS
with explicit commands (hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /datasets/cnn_dailymail, hdfs
dfs -put … /datasets/cnn_dailymail/); Spark then reads from
hdfs://localhost:9000/datasets/…. Colab is not used for the Spark and
HDFS analysis. The top-m sentences chosen by Stage 1 are written back to
HDFS and read from there to build the Stage 2 prompts. Extra libraries
(NLTK, rouge-score, Hugging Face Transformers) and all versions are
listed in the first cell. LLM generation runs in the VM if the models
fit; otherwise on the host or a GPU machine using article IDs and text
read from HDFS, and the outputs are loaded back into HDFS as a table
(documented, never described as VM inference).

## 3. Proposed Analyses

|                      | Stage 1: ML sentence selector (Spark MLlib)                                                                                                                                                                    | Stage 2: prompt injection and LLM generation                                                                                                                                                   |
|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Pattern              | One article sentence                                                                                                                                                                                           | One generated summary (article × model × strategy × task)                                                                                                                                      |
| Target               | 1 if the sentence is in the greedy oracle extractive summary built from the reference highlights, else 0                                                                                                       | 1 if the output satisfies the requested constraint (deterministic checker), else 0                                                                                                             |
| Features / input     | Numeric: sentence position, relative position, length in words, number of digits, number of capitalized words, TF-IDF similarity to the article, similarity to the first sentence, article length in sentences | Prompt = control instruction + article text or the top-m (m = 8) sentences chosen by the best Stage 1 model                                                                                    |
| Algorithms / systems | Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient-Boosted Trees; hyperparameters tuned with CrossValidator; non-ML Lead-k baseline                                                                                  | FLAN-T5-base, Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, BART-large-CNN (summarization baseline)                                                                                                                   |
| Strategies / tasks   | Top-k selection (k = 4, 5) also gives a pure-ML summary                                                                                                                                                        | Strategies: zero-shot, explicit constraints, few-shot, ML-guided (top-m injected), Lead-m-guided (control). Tasks: exactly 4 sentences; exactly 100 words; at most 50 words; exactly 5 bullets |
| NLP tools            | NLTK (sentence splitting); Spark ML TF-IDF; rouge-score (oracle labels, ROUGE)                                                                                                                                 | NLTK and custom validators (counting rules fixed and unit-tested before the test run); rouge-score; Transformers                                                                               |
| Evaluation           | ROC-AUC, PR-AUC and F1 (imbalanced classes) on the test split; ROUGE-1/2/L of the top-k summaries                                                                                                              | Compliance rate and count error per task; ROUGE; manual factuality check on a fixed subset; truncation, latency and tokens; paired comparisons with confidence intervals                       |

**How the stages connect:** Stage 1 output (ranked sentences, in HDFS)
is the input of the ML-guided prompts in Stage 2. The pure-ML top-4
summary is also compared with the LLMs on the same 50 held-out articles.

### Research questions

- **Stage 1:** Can sentences that enter the reference summary be
  predicted from positional and lexical features? Do ensemble methods
  beat Logistic Regression?

- **Stage 2:** Which model and prompt strategy most reliably meets exact
  sentence, word and bullet constraints? Does explicit wording or
  few-shot beat zero-shot?

- **Combined:** Does injecting ML-selected sentences improve compliance,
  ROUGE and speed versus full-article prompts and versus Lead-m,
  especially for models with short context? Does higher compliance cost
  summary quality?

